<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Territory of the Comoros</h1><p class="st-meta">1946 CE – 1975 CE · capital Dzaoudzi; later Moroni</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The French overseas territory of the Comoro Islands, whose split vote on independence left Mayotte French.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comoro-islands">Comoro Islands</a> had been attached to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-madagascar">French Madagascar</a> since 1912. In 1946 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> made them a separate overseas territory, the Territoire des Comores, with its own elected assembly and deputies in the French parliament, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzaoudzi">Dzaoudzi</a> on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-mayotte-and-dependencies">Mayotte</a> as the administrative capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The islands' economy rested on vanilla, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cloves">cloves</a>, copra and ylang-ylang essential oil, largely controlled by French companies and local elites. Internal autonomy was granted in 1961 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-mohamed-cheikh">Said Mohamed Cheikh</a>. The capital was moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzaoudzi">Dzaoudzi</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moroni">Moroni</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grande-comore">Grande Comore</a> in the 1960s, a decision resented on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-mayotte-and-dependencies">Mayotte</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 1974 referendum three islands voted for independence while <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-mayotte-and-dependencies">Mayotte</a> voted to stay French. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmed-abdallah">Ahmed Abdallah</a> declared independence on 6 July 1975, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> kept Mayotte, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comoro-islands">Comoros</a> still claim.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/territory-of-the-comoros.jpg" alt="Territory of the Comoros" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_France.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1946 CE – 1975 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dzaoudzi; later Moroni</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Said Mohamed Cheikh, Ahmed Abdallah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Comorian, French, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/comoros">Comoros</a>, <a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Territoire des Comores</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1960&amp;focus=territory-of-the-comoros" aria-label="Territory of the Comoros on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5870 2614 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Territory of the Comoros lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6190 2870L5054 728M6190 2870L6243 2910"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M6243 2910h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6190 2870h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6190 2870h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1960&amp;focus=territory-of-the-comoros">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
